CONTEXT
  subject: William Brendel — engineering executive, 2x founder, PhD in AI.
  product: A chat box on his personal site, william-brendel.com, that answers
           as him. Sections come from his resume, his stories, and his stated
           views.
  audience: Recruiters, hiring managers, founders, engineers, and investors
            who landed on his page. They type interview questions, not
            documentation lookups.

INPUT
  $section:
    description:
      One section of a first-person document about William. Has a header and
      body content. The content is written as "I did X" — it is his voice.
    format: text/markdown

OUTPUT
  $rows:
    description: JSON array, no markdown, no preamble.
    format: JSON
    schema:
      [
        {
          "register": "string", // one of: behavioral, values, factual, logistics, narrative
          "angle": "string",    // one of: What, Why, How, When, Who, Where
          "question": "string",
          "variants": ["string"],
          "anchors": ["string"]
        }
      ]

GOAL
  description:
    Generate retrieval rows so that a visitor's question finds this section.
    Work backward: facts first, register and angle second, question and
    downstream fields last.

    The single most important job: a visitor almost never uses the words in
    the section. They ask "tell me about a time you failed" and the section
    says "we overbuilt features before validating demand." Your generated
    questions and variants are the bridge. If you only rephrase the section's
    own vocabulary, you have done nothing.

  REASONING:

    # Extract facts

    Extract every discrete fact or claim from the section. A fact is discrete
    if a visitor could independently ask about it.

    Critical: only use what is in $section. Never add outside knowledge about
    William, even if it seems obviously true. A plausible invented fact is
    indistinguishable from a hallucination at review time, and this corpus
    speaks in his name.

    # Assign a register

    The register is what KIND of question this fact answers. It drives the
    phrasings a visitor would actually use:

      - behavioral — a story about something he did. Visitors reach these
        with "tell me about a time you…", "describe a situation where…",
        "give me an example of…". The classic interview stems.
      - values     — a stated view, principle, or preference. Reached with
        "how do you think about…", "what's your take on…", "do you believe…".
      - factual    — a role, date, number, employer, credential, outcome.
        Reached with "what did you do at…", "how long were you…", "how many…".
      - logistics  — contact, availability, what he wants next, compensation,
        location. Reached with "how do I reach you", "are you open to…".
      - narrative  — context or framing that supports a story without being
        the story. Reached obliquely; generate fewer rows for these.

    A section can produce rows of different registers. A story about a
    decision usually yields both behavioral rows (the situation) and values
    rows (what it says about how he works).

    # Derive angles

    For each fact, identify applicable angles: What (defines), Why (reason or
    cause), How (process or approach), When (timing or condition), Who
    (people involved), Where (place or company).

    Stay within the angle — a What row must not explain Why. If blending feels
    natural, split into two rows. One row per (fact, angle) pair.

    # Compose rows

    FOREACH angle of fact

      # Derive the question

      The single most natural question a visitor would type to get this fact.

        - Address him directly, second person: "what did YOU do at Snap",
          not "what did William do at Snap". Visitors talk to the box.
        - Be specific to this section. Test: strip the header — would the
          question still identify which section it came from? If it could
          equally match a sibling section, it is too broad. Rewrite.
        - Naturalness gate: would a recruiter or engineer actually type this?
            Wrong: "What was the technical debt reduction percentage?"
            Right: "What did you actually fix at iScribeHealth?"
            Wrong: "What is the subject's position on work-life balance?"
            Right: "How do you feel about 80-hour weeks?"
        - Usefulness gate: the answer must tell them something the header
          alone would not. If it just restates the header, skip the row.

      # Derive anchors

      Short search fragments, no question marks — what someone would type into
      a search box rather than ask aloud.

        - Maximum 2 anchors.
        - 2 to 5 meaningful terms each.
        - Range from broad to specific.
        - Prefer proper nouns where the fact has them: employer, product,
          technology, credential. "snap ar filter decision", "iscribe soc 2".

      # Derive variants

      Other ways a visitor would reach this same fact and angle. This is where
      most retrieval recall is won or lost.

        - Ownership rule: every variant must be fully answered by THIS row's
          fact and angle. If another row would answer it better, discard it —
          a misplaced variant steals recall from the correct row.
        - Keep variants in the row's angle. A How row's variants ask How.
        - 3 to 7 variants. Within them:

          - IF register is behavioral, THEN at least 2 variants must use
            standard interview stems, phrased generically enough that someone
            who has never read this section would type them:
              "tell me about a time you failed"
              "describe a conflict with a colleague"
              "give me an example of a hard decision you made"
              "what's your biggest mistake"
            This is the highest-value line in this prompt. A story about
            overbuilding before validating demand MUST carry "tell me about a
            time you failed" and "what's a mistake you've made" as variants,
            even though neither phrase appears anywhere in the section.

          - IF register is values, THEN at least 2 variants must be the
            abstract form of the question, with no reference to the specific
            situation:
              "how do you handle disagreement"
              "what's your management style"
              "how do you think about technical debt"

          - IF register is factual, THEN at least 1 variant must be the
            casual form ("what'd you do at Amazon", "how long were you at
            Snap") and at least 1 the fragment form ("snap tenure",
            "amazon role").

          - At least 1 variant across every register must be a sentence
            fragment or search-style phrase, not a grammatical question.

          - At least 1 variant must avoid every distinctive noun the section
            uses. If the section says "beauty score filter", that variant must
            not contain "beauty", "score", or "filter". This forces coverage of
            visitors who describe the situation rather than name it.

        - Do NOT generate variants that are the section's own sentences
          lightly reworded. Those already retrieve well from the body vectors.
          Your job is the phrasings the body will never match.

      # Coverage check

      Before emitting, read your rows against this: if a visitor asked the
      five most obvious interview questions this section could answer, would
      at least one row match each? If a standard question has no row, add it.
